# Security

These rules always apply. Directory-level guidance may add controls. It must not silently weaken tenancy, authorization, secret handling, or audit requirements.

## Tenancy and authorization

- Deny access by default. Every tenant-owned operation must be scoped to the authenticated organization.
- Never treat a client-supplied `organizationId` as sufficient authorization.
- Do not make security decisions solely in the browser.
- Protect against IDOR and confused-deputy behavior in integrations.

## Untrusted input

- Validate all untrusted input with Zod at system boundaries.
- Treat SBOMs, archives, webhooks, vulnerability feeds, headers, URLs, files, and external API responses as untrusted.
- Validate CycloneDX documents before parsing. Enforce upload and body-size limits.
- Generate SHA-256 hashes for uploaded evidence. Store private artifacts in private object storage.
- Guard any remote fetch against SSRF (allowlists, blocked link-local/metadata ranges, no user-controlled internal URLs).

## Secrets and configuration

- Never hardcode secrets, credentials, tokens, API keys, private URLs, or environment-specific values.
- Read `process.env` only in the typed config package.
- Encrypt stored external credentials. Prefer short-lived provider tokens.
- Keep development adapters disabled in production.
- Users may later supply their own AI key or local endpoint. Keys must never be hardcoded. AI is optional and must not be required.

## Logging, evidence, and disclosure

- Add request and correlation identifiers.
- Never log authorization headers, cookies, API tokens, GitHub tokens, raw SBOMs, private source code, or complete feed payloads.
- Do not claim compliance, certification, exploitability, or remediation without supporting evidence.
- Report vulnerabilities privately per [SECURITY.md](../../SECURITY.md). Do not open public issues that disclose exploits.

## Tests

Security-sensitive changes require regression tests. Do not add exploit payloads or attack scripts.
